commit c2bdda0d3d464351dfbb77ca0465df696d7d9b2e
Author: ai.teammate <ai.teammate@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Tue Oct 6 23:00:05 2026 +0300

    test(1337): anchor countdown drain on the settle-notice predicate, not stream silence

    The drained wait was waitForOutput(settleMs: 500) — a silence settle
    that returns once ~1s of raw-stream quiet passes. The scenario staggers
    its ten sleeps exactly 1s apart (3..12s), so the gaps BETWEEN settle
    notices sit on that threshold; on the loaded v1.0.520 runner one
    stretched gap (job 7 -> 8) satisfied the silence rule and returned the
    capture mid-drain with only {1..7} settled. waitForOutput also returns
    silently on timeout, so expiry never surfaced diagnostics.

    Replace it with waitForRaw — a new predicate-anchored raw-stream wait
    on the harness (reuses pollUntil; throws with screen/raw-tail
    diagnostics on expiry) — polling settledJobNumbers(raw).length == 10
    with a 120s slow-runner ceiling. The proof set moves to the harness
    top-level (maskedValueRow convention) and is unit-pinned in the default
    suite: distinct-set tolerance for repaint re-emits, number-prefix
    tolerance for CI-load mid-id fragmentation, and rejection of the exact
    truncated {1..7} capture.

